WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:02.760
Hi everyone, my name's Adam Kocoloski with IBM Cloud

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:04.700
and I'm here to talk to you today about data lakes

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:06.740
- what they are, how you use one,

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:09.240
and the kind of things you ought to be thinking about as you set one up

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.580
to power your applications and

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:14.500
create more intelligent experiences for users.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:18.060
So, data lakes exist because we're all awash with data

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.450
and we've got systems of record,

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:22.320
we've got systems of engagement,

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:26.300
we've got streaming data, we've got batch data internal, external data,

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:29.360
and it's really a combination of these different kinds of data sources

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:32.080
that leads us to get powerful insights

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:33.700
about what our users are doing,

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.780
about the way the world is working around us,

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:38.800
and leads us to develop more intelligent applications.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:42.860
Data lakes start by collecting all those different types of data sources

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:44.920
through a common ingestion framework

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:47.920
and that ingestion framework is something that typically wants to be able

00:00:47.929 --> 00:00:50.740
to support a diverse array of different types of data,

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:52.559
and it wants to kind of standardize

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:55.920
and centralize all that stuff into a common storage repository.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:57.600
That's not always required,

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.940
but typically you don't want to be analyzing the source data directly,

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:02.840
you want to be able to take a copy of it,

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:07.160
so that you've got the flexibility to do the kind of things you need to do with that data.

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:08.880
And speaking of that,

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:12.640
the data typically doesn't common a form where you can use it right out  of the box.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:17.500
There's a lot of data cleansing and data preparation that's required.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:26.020
There is often times the ability to, or the
requirement to create new features,

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:28.060
something we call feature extraction,

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:31.920
combinations of different types of data that need to be

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:35.700
pulled together in order to create the right

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:40.700
sort of bits of information to analyze.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:44.320
And once you cleanse that data, prep
the data,

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:48.180
model the right kind of features for your analysis,

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:52.060
then you get to the fun part - which is actually going in and doing the machine learning model training

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:56.000
and doing your advanced analytics.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:02.680
And each of these steps is typically creating new derived data sets

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:04.980
that tie back to the original one.

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:07.320
And that relationship is a really important thing to capture,

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:09.740
because, let's say, there was a problem with one of your data sources.

00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:12.160
You know there was a correction that needed to be made.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:15.840
You need to understand how that flows through the entire pipeline

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.140
of more refined data sets and models that you're producing,

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:20.940
so that you can go back and correct it.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:23.140
And that's what this governance stuff comes
into play.

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:26.640
This is something that's really you know infused
at every step of the journey.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:31.630
It means collecting meta data, you know data
about your data, you know the right kinds

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:35.780
of information about the tables in your data
sets and how they relate to one another.

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:40.600
It means being able to enforce policies so
that as an organization we use the data the

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:45.110
way it's meant to be used, the way it's intended
to be used, the way it's acceptable to be

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:47.060
used to drive the business forward.

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:50.670
That's really something that can't be bolted
on after the fact that something has to be

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:53.420
present throughout the entire life cycle.

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:57.410
If we stop here, we haven't really changed
anything.

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:01.490
It's only by getting these insights that were
producing in this data lake back out into

00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:07.170
the real world that were able to you know
deliver on the business promise of these data

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:11.730
lakes that that we're all investing in and
that's where this apply step comes in.

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:14.000
This can take a few different forms.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:19.950
You might be you know building simply dashboards
That are helping business executives make

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:24.590
smarter decisions about where to take the
business forward with new projects to invest

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:25.700
in.

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:31.400
Or you might be building smarter applications
that are able to make intelligent recommendations

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:38.030
to the users of those apps based on you know
historical purchased data.

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:45.250
Increasingly we're also seeing a lot of process
automation where an intelligent model can

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:51.110
smooth over some typically manual business
processes and create a more intelligent experience

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:58.069
and based on the sort of rich data driven
understanding of the problem at hand.

00:03:58.069 --> 00:04:02.099
And really this whole process iterates back,
right.

00:04:02.099 --> 00:04:07.580
Those more intelligent applications, they
end up generating new data and the cycle continues.

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:12.620
And so that in a nutshell at a very high level
is what a day lake does.

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:17.660
Some of you may have heard us talk about "the
ladder to AI", the "AI ladder", and we talk

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:21.320
about that - we talk about collecting data.

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:25.820
We talk about organizing data.

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:29.410
We talk about analyzing.

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:32.720
And we talk about infusing.

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:38.660
And really those four steps on this ladder
are things that you can see represented throughout

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:40.830
this data lake environment.

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:44.780
Clearly over here we're doing a lot of collection
of these individual sources of data.

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:49.190
This data preparation and feature extraction
step into governed fashion is absolutely what

00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:52.710
we mean by the organizing of data.

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:55.930
ML model training is a key example of data
analysis.

00:04:55.930 --> 00:05:01.090
And we talk about infusing the insights from
the data lake into the applications, that's

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:02.830
really this last step here.

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:08.220
And so, there is very much a clear linkage
between climbing this AI ladder and a data

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:11.750
lake as a vehicle that can help you make that
journey.

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:12.750
Thanks for watching.

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:13.750
If you have any questions or comments, please
drop us a line below.

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:15.780
